# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Expo loads `.env` automatically; only keys
# prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ reach the app bundle — and they are readable by anyone
# who has the app, so nothing secret belongs in this file.

# Where the app finds the Next.js API (oxvercity, port 3300).
#
# Leave it unset when Metro and the API are on the same LAN address: the app
# derives the host from Expo's manifest, which is what `npm start` gives you on
# an ordinary laptop.
#
# Set it in the two cases where that derivation cannot work:
#
#   1. `npm run start:tunnel`. Metro is served from an exp.direct forwarder that
#      carries Metro's port and nothing else, so there is no port 3300 behind
#      that hostname. The app now says so instead of hanging, but it still needs
#      a real URL here.
#
#   2. This machine is a ChromeOS Linux (Crostini) container. Its address,
#      100.115.92.x, is private to the Chromebook — a phone on the same Wi-Fi
#      has no route to it. Forward ports 8081 and 3300 in
#      Settings -> Advanced -> Developers -> Linux -> Port forwarding, then use
#      the Chromebook's own LAN address here and in Expo Go's "Enter URL
#      manually" box (exp://<that address>:8081).
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.20:3300
#
# Staging or production is just the deployed origin:
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://sxccaa.org
